<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>Albumin in the urine (286 mg/g) and an eGFR of 54 by the race-free CKD-EPI 2021 equation in a 49-year-old woman with high blood pressure readings awaiting ambulatory confirmation, left ventricular hypertrophy on ECG and protein on a dipstick a year ago. Please advise on the cause, any further tests, kidney-protective treatment and her blood pressure target, APOL1 testing, and a monitoring plan that fits a schedule of travel most weeks. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Within two weeks, on the day of her two-week visit, so that she makes one trip. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>Is this hypertensive kidney disease, or does she need tests for another cause, or a kidney biopsy?</item><item>Which medicines do you advise for her blood pressure and kidneys, and what is her blood pressure target?</item><item>Should she start an SGLT2 inhibitor without diabetes?</item><item>Should she be offered APOL1 genetic testing, and how would the result be used?</item><item>How should we monitor potassium, creatinine and albumin, given that she travels most weeks?</item><item>What is her risk of kidney failure, and when should she see you again?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Madam Walker, 49, is seen at the request of Dr. Okonjo-Bell on the day of her two-week visit. She asked to be addressed as Madam Walker. Her ambulatory monitor showed a 24-hour mean of 149/94, daytime 154/98 and night 140/88, and her home readings average 152/96. Her office has sent last year&apos;s executive physical: creatinine 1.18 mg/dL and protein 1+ on a dipstick. She has had morning headaches on some days. She has no visible blood in the urine, no foamy urine she has noticed, no swelling, rash, joint pain, mouth ulcers or kidney stones, and takes no NSAIDs, supplements or herbal products. No known family kidney disease; both parents died when she was a child. She asked plainly whether she will need dialysis and said she would take whatever medicines were needed but would not give up her travel. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Madam C. J. Walker was born Sarah Breedlove on December 23, 1867, in Delta, Louisiana, the daughter of Owen and Minerva Anderson Breedlove. Both parents had been enslaved on a Madison Parish plantation, and she was their first child born into freedom. [D]</item><item>Both her parents had died by the time she was seven, and she went to live with an older sister and moved across the river to Vicksburg, Mississippi. She married Moses McWilliams at fourteen, had her only child, Lelia (later A&apos;Lelia), and was widowed at twenty. She then moved to St. Louis, where she worked as a laundress and cook, sometimes for as little as a dollar a day. [D]</item><item>While working as a laundress she began to lose her hair, a problem many Black women of her time shared. Her biographers attribute it to scalp disease such as dandruff, poor diet, a hard life and harsh washing products, in an era when most homes lacked indoor plumbing. In 1904 she began using and selling another Black woman&apos;s hair grower as a sales agent, then developed her own sulfur-containing ointments and shampoos to treat scalp disease. [D]</item><item>In Denver she married Charles Joseph Walker, a newspaper advertising salesman (in January 1906, or 1905 in one account), and began calling herself Madam Walker, in part to avoid the familiar forms of address then used for Black women. She launched her own hair-care line there, set up headquarters and a training school for her agents in Pittsburgh in 1908, and moved the company to Indianapolis in 1910. She trained thousands of sales agents, &quot;hair culturists,&quot; who earned a share of the profits. She kept the name after the marriage ended. [D]</item><item>As her wealth grew she became a philanthropist and activist: she gave to the YWCA and paid tuition for students at Tuskegee Institute, and in 1915 she sued over discrimination at an Indianapolis theater. [D]</item><item>The physical demands she placed on herself undermined her health, and she suffered from high blood pressure. She went for a rest cure at Hot Springs, Arkansas, in 1916, but otherwise did not slow her pace. [D]</item></list><paragraph>4 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

