<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.8"/>
    <code code="51848-0" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation note"/>
    <title>Assessment</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item>Chronic kidney disease G3a A2 (N18.31). Albuminuria and a reduced eGFR present for more than a year: CKD G3a A2, high risk on the KDIGO heat map. Long-standing hypertension with left ventricular hypertrophy and retinopathy, a bland sediment without hematuria, albuminuria below 300 mg/g and normal-sized kidneys make hypertensive kidney disease the most likely cause. A primary glomerular disease is less likely, and a biopsy is not needed now. Her 5-year kidney failure risk is 1.6% (4-variable KFRE): low, and treatment can lower it further. The race-free equation stages her correctly; the older equation with a race coefficient would have reported an eGFR of about 60. [S]</item><item>Hypertension with chronic kidney disease (I12.9). Stage 2 hypertension confirmed by ambulatory monitoring, with a non-dipping pattern. With albuminuria, a renin-angiotensin blocker at the maximum tolerated dose is the foundation, chosen for the kidney disease and not for her race; an ARB avoids the cough of an ACE inhibitor, which matters for her work. Target below 130/80, and a systolic near 120 if she tolerates it with standardized measurement. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Data reviewed: Dr. Okonjo-Bell&apos;s intake note and results: creatinine 1.22 mg/dL, eGFR 54 at intake and 1.21 mg/dL, eGFR 55 today (CKD-EPI 2021, race-free); potassium 4.4 mmol/L; urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio 286 mg/g at intake and 294 mg/g today; urinalysis 1+ protein without blood, bland sediment; calcium 9.4 mg/dL, phosphate 3.6 mg/dL, parathyroid hormone 58 pg/mL, 25-hydroxyvitamin D 31 ng/mL; A1c 5.4%; LDL 174 mg/dL; hemoglobin 12.4 g/dL. ECG: left ventricular hypertrophy by voltage. Kidney ultrasound this morning: right kidney 10.4 cm, left 10.6 cm, normal cortical thickness, mildly increased echogenicity, no hydronephrosis, stones or cysts. Ambulatory and home readings as above; clinic 160/100 today. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.10" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="18776-5" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Plan of care note"/>
    <title>Plan of Treatment</title>
    <text><list><item>Losartan 50 mg each morning, increased to 100 mg after two weeks if potassium is below 5.5 mmol/L and creatinine has risen by less than 30%. [S]</item><item>Empagliflozin 10 mg daily for kidney protection, indicated at her eGFR and albuminuria without diabetes; expect a small early dip in eGFR. [S]</item><item>Sick-day rules: hold losartan and empagliflozin on days of vomiting, diarrhea or not eating, and restart after a day of normal eating and drinking. [S]</item><item>Potassium and creatinine two to four weeks after each start or increase, then every six months with the urine albumin; booking the draws on her days at home is acceptable. [S]</item><item>Amlodipine as the next medicine if blood pressure stays above goal on the maximum losartan dose; no potassium-based salt substitutes. [S]</item><item>Hepatitis B, hepatitis C and HIV tests now, as part of the evaluation of new CKD, and hepatitis B vaccination if she is not immune. [S]</item><item>APOL1 genotyping may be offered with genetic counseling: two high-risk variants raise the lifetime risk of kidney disease and help explain it, but the result would not change today&apos;s treatment; it is her choice whether and when to test. [S]</item><item>No biopsy now; reconsider for hematuria, albuminuria rising above 1,000 mg/g despite treatment, or an unexplained fall in eGFR of more than 30%. [S]</item><item>Avoid NSAIDs, and tell any clinician about her kidneys before contrast dye. [S]</item><item>Discussed with Dr. Okonjo-Bell after the visit. The diagnosis, the low 5-year risk and each medicine were explained to Madam Walker with the numbers, and she agreed to start losartan and empagliflozin today. APOL1 testing was explained and written information given; she will decide later. [S]</item><item>Follow-up: Nephrology review in about six months, by video if she prefers, or sooner for potassium above 5.5 mmol/L, a fall in eGFR of more than 30%, albuminuria rising, or blood pressure not at goal on three medicines; Dr. Okonjo-Bell continues care and monitoring. [S]</item></list></text>

